1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
In this lesson, you'll build a project with a bit more scope.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
We'll build a compact version of an AI researcher, in this case, an essay writer.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Let's get to it.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
So we're going to create an essay writer.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This is going to be broken down into a few different sub-steps.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
So first, we're going to generate a plan for the essay.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
This is going to happen once up front.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Then based on that plan, we're going to do some research.

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
This involves calling tovilly and getting back some documents.

10
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
From there, we're going to go into generate.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
And in generate, we're going to follow the plan using the documents that we researched

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
and we're going to write the essay.

13
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
From there, we're either going to finish or we're going to continue.

14
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
If we continue, we're going to go into our reflect node or we're going to generate a critique of the current essay.

15
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Then based on that critique, we're going to do another research step with tovilly

16
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
and get another set of documents.

17
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
We're going to append those to the existing set of documents and go all the way back to the generate step.

18
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
We're going to, once again, try to write this essay.

19
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
And we're going to continue this until some criteria is met and we exit.

20
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Let's see this in code.

21
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
We're going to start with, again, importing all our environment variables.

22
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
We're then going to make some standard imports and set up our in-memory SQLite check planter.

23
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Now we're going to create the agent state and it's going to be a little bit more complicated than before.

24
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,000
So we're going to want to keep track of a bunch of different things.

25
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
First, we're going to keep track of the task.

26
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
This is the human input. This is what we're trying to write an essay about.

27
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Then we're going to have a key to keep track of the plan.

28
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
This is the plan that planning agent will generate.

29
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Then we've got a key for the draft.

30
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
This is a draft of the essay.

31
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
And then we've got a key for the critique part.

32
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
So the critique agent will populate this key.

33
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
We're also going to add in content.

34
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,000
This is a key to keep track of the list of documents that Tavilly has researched and come back with.

35
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,000
And then we're going to have two arguments that keep track of how many times you've gone through this.

36
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:22,000
So revision number, which is going to keep track of the number of revisions we've made and max revisions.

37
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Which is keeping track of the maximum revisions that we want to make.

38
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
We're going to use those last two things in the criteria to decide whether to stop or not.

39
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Let's create our model.

40
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
We're going to be using OpenAI.

41
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
And now let's write some prompts for our agents.

42
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
First, we have this planning prompt.

43
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
This is the prompt for the LLM that's going to write out a plan for our essay.

44
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Next, we have the writer prompt.

45
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:02,000
This is the one who's writing the essay given all the content that was researched.

46
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
We now have the reflection prompt.

47
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:11,000
This is the prompt that's going to control how we're critiquing the draft of the essay.

48
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
We now have a prompt that we're going to use for the agents that's doing research after the planning step.

49
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,000
So given a plan, we're going to generate a bunch of queries and pass these to Tavili.

50
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
So this is the prompt that does that.

51
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
And then we also have this research critique prompt.

52
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
So after we've made the critique, we're going to generate a list of queries to pass to Tavili.

53
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
So this is slightly different than the planning prompt just because it's working on a critique versus a plan.

54
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,000
But it serves a similar purpose.

55
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:52,000
For generating these lists of queries to pass to Tavili, we're going to use function calling to ensure that we get back a list of strings from the language model.

56
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:04,000
In order to do that, we're going to set up this pedantic model that just represents the result that we want to get back from the language model, which in this case is a list of strings.

57
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:11,000
We're going to import the Tavili client instead of the tool because we're working with it in a slightly unconventional way.

58
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Now we can start to create all our different agents, all our different nodes.

59
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Let's go in order.

60
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,000
So first, we're going to start with the node or agent that does the planning.

61
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
What this node is going to do is going to take in the state.

62
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
It's then going to create a list of messages.

63
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:30,000
One of them is going to be the planning prompt. That's going to be the system message.

64
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
We're then going to create a human message that is the task that we want to do.

65
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
We're going to pass that to the model.

66
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:37,000
We're going to get it back a message.

67
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,000
And then we're going to take the content of that message and set that equal to plan.

68
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,000
And so this is going to update the plan key to be this response.

69
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:54,000
The next node that we're going to want to do is the agent that takes in the plan and does some research.

70
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
So for this, we're going to create the research plan node.

71
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
This is going to take in the agent state and we're first going to generate some queries.

72
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
So we're going to do model and this is the open AI model.

73
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
And then we're going to say with structured output and put queries right there.

74
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
And so this is basically saying that the response of what we're going to invoke this with

75
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
is going to be that pedantic object that we had, which has that list of queries.

76
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
So we're going to call and vote and we're going to call and vote on a list of messages.

77
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:27,000
We've got this research planning prompt and then we pass in this human message, this task.

78
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
And so for this part, we're generating the queries based on the task.

79
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:38,000
We're then going to get our list of current documents, the content documents that we're going to use to write this essay.

80
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,000
So we're going to look in the state for content if it exists or if it doesn't exist.

81
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
We're just going to create an empty list of documents.

82
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
We're then going to loop over the queries that we generated and search for them into Ville.

83
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
We're going to get back the results and we're going to append them to the content.

84
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:05,000
We're then going to return this content key equal to the content that is the result of the original content plus the accumulated content.

85
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Okay, so we've made a plan and we've done some initial research.

86
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Now it's time to write the first draft.

87
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
So for the generation node, the first thing we're going to do is prepare the content.

88
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,000
So we're going to take this list of strings and join them into one big one.

89
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:26,000
We're then going to create the user message where we're going to combine the task and the plan.

90
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,000
So we create a user message that's basically like, this is my task and then here's my plan.

91
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
The plan, remember, this is what we generated two steps before.

92
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
The task is the original user input.

93
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
We're then going to create a list of messages.

94
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,000
This is a system message with the writer prompt where we format in this content.

95
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
We format in the documents that we fetched.

96
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And then we're going to pass in this user message, which is a combination of the task and the plan.

97
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:57,000
We're going to pass this to the model and get back a response.

98
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
We're then going to update two things in the state.

99
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
First, we're going to update the draft.

100
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,000
So we're going to take their response from the model and use that as the new draft version.

101
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Then we're going to update the revision number.

102
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:11,000
So this keeps track of how many revisions we've made.

103
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:18,000
And so here, we're going to add one to the current revision number in the state.

104
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:23,000
After generating, we now need a reflection node.

105
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:28,000
This reflection node is going to take the reflection prompt as a system prompt.

106
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
And it's going to take the draft.

107
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
This is what we just generated.

108
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
We're going to have these two messages and we're going to pass them into the model and get back a response.

109
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:41,000
And this is going to generate the critique that we're going to update the state.

110
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:47,000
The last agent we need is the agent that's going to take the critique and do some research.

111
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,000
So we're going to do a very similar thing that we did with the research plan node.

112
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:52,000
We're going to take this model.

113
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
We're going to do with structured output with queries, so that's returning queries.

114
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:57,000
We're going to invoke it.

115
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,000
This time, we're passing in the research critique prompt.

116
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
